[Fact:negativeBases:The ↑ operator and negative bases]
Expressions such as 'X↑Y' give an 'Invalid argument' error when X is negative,
even when Y is an integer. For example:

<code>PRINT (-1)↑2</code>

This is because the #R$3851 routine computes the value as EXP (Y*LN X), and
LN X is undefined for negative values of X.

[Fact:comparingNumbers:Comparing numbers can overflow (or underflow)]
Numeric comparisons work by subtracting one number from the other (see the
#R$300F call at #R$3554). This means that:

<code>PRINT 1e38&gt;-1e38</code>

gives a 'Number too big' error instead of the expected '1'. Any comparison
whose difference exceeds the largest number that can be represented in
floating-point form (about 1.7e38) fails the same way.

For the same reason, slightly different very small numbers are regarded as
equal when compared (because their difference rounds down to zero). For
example:

<code>PRINT 5e-39,6e-39</code>

prints two different values, but:

<code>PRINT 5e-39=6e-39</code>

prints '1' (as if the values were equal).
